1️⃣ How We Use Indexing in Spring Boot?

Spring Boot itself does not create indexes automatically (except primary key).
Indexes are defined at:

✅ Database level (recommended)

✅ JPA entity level (optional)

✅ 1. Using Index with JPA Entity

Example:

@Entity
@Table(
    name = "users",
    indexes = {
        @Index(name = "idx_created_role", columnList = "created_date, role_type")
    }
)
public class User {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private LocalDate createdDate;
    private String roleType;
    private Double salary;
}

👉 When Spring Boot runs (if ddl-auto=update/create),
Hibernate creates the index in MySQL.

🔥 Interview Point

Indexes are used to optimize WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY queries in Spring Boot applications.

2️⃣ How Spring Boot Uses Index Internally?

Suppose you write:

List<User> users = userRepository
    .findByCreatedDateAndRoleType(date, "ADMIN");

Spring Data JPA generates:

SELECT *
FROM users
WHERE created_date = ?
AND role_type = ?

If index exists:

(created_date, role_type)

👉 MySQL optimizer automatically uses it.

Spring Boot does nothing special — DB handles optimization.

3️⃣ Covering Index in Spring Boot

If your query is:

@Query("SELECT u.salary FROM User u WHERE u.createdDate = :date AND u.roleType = :role")
List<Double> getSalary(...);

If index is:

(created_date, role_type, salary)

Then:

✔ No table lookup
✔ Faster response
✔ Less I/O

Very useful in high-traffic APIs.

4️⃣ Checking Index Usage in Spring Boot

You can test in MySQL:

EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM users WHERE role_type = 'ADMIN';

Or enable Hibernate SQL logs:

spring.jpa.show-sql=true
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.format_sql=true

Then copy query → run EXPLAIN manually.

5️⃣ Selectivity in Real Spring Boot Project

Bad example:

findByGender(String gender)

If gender has only 2 values → low selectivity.

Index may not help much.

Better indexing candidates:

email (unique)

user_id

order_id

transaction_id

6️⃣ Composite Index Design in Spring Boot APIs

Suppose your API frequently does:

findByRoleTypeAndCreatedDateBetween(...)

Then best index:

(role_type, created_date)

Because:

role_type → equality

created_date → range

🔥 Perfect composite order.

7️⃣ Real Backend Optimization Example

Imagine:

Page<User> findByRoleType(String role, Pageable pageable);

Spring generates:

SELECT * FROM users
WHERE role_type = ?
ORDER BY id
LIMIT 10 OFFSET 0;

If no index → full table scan ❌

Better:

(role_type, id)

Now:

✔ WHERE optimized
✔ ORDER BY optimized
✔ Pagination faster

8️⃣ Very Important (Production Level)

In high traffic systems:

Avoid too many indexes (slows insert/update)

Index only frequently filtered columns

Monitor slow query log

Use EXPLAIN before adding index

🎯 Interview-Ready Answer (Spring Boot)

In Spring Boot, indexing is defined at the database level or using @Index in JPA.
 The database optimizer automatically uses indexes for repository queries. Proper composite index design based on query patterns
  significantly improves API performance.

