👉 When Spring Boot runs (if `ddl-auto=update/create`),

if i do ddl auto none then what will be

Good question 👍 very common Spring Boot interview point.

You’re asking:

If I set spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=none, what will happen?

🔹 What is ddl-auto?

Property:

spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update

Controls how Hibernate handles database schema generation.

Options:

Value	Meaning
create	Drops and recreates tables every startup
create-drop	Create at start, drop at shutdown
update	Updates schema without deleting data
validate	Checks schema matches entity
none	Does nothing
🔥 If You Set:
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=none
👉 Hibernate will NOT:

Create tables

Update tables

Create indexes

Validate schema

It will completely ignore schema generation.

⚠️ What Does That Mean Practically?

If your database does not already have:

tables

columns

indexes

Then your application will fail at runtime when querying.

Example error:

Table 'users' doesn't exist
✅ When Do We Use none?

In production environments.

Because:

Schema changes are handled by:

Liquibase

Flyway

Manual SQL scripts

You don’t want Hibernate modifying production DB automatically

🔥 Important for Your Index Question

If you define:

@Index(name = "idx_created_role", columnList = "created_date, role_type")

But:

ddl-auto=none

👉 Hibernate will NOT create that index.

You must manually create it:

CREATE INDEX idx_created_role
ON users(created_date, role_type);
🎯 Real Project Best Practice
Development:
ddl-auto=update
Production:
ddl-auto=none

And use:

Flyway
or

Liquibase

🧠 Senior-Level Interview Answer

When ddl-auto is set to none, Hibernate does not perform any schema management.
Database structure must be maintained externally. This is recommended in production environments.